    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="12" relname="span">P37, this is a good synopsis of what Raskin states as intuitiveness being equal to familiarity.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="3" relname="volitional-cause">We learn things,</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="18" relname="span">they become more familiar,</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="17" relname="span">therefore they become intuitive.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="6" relname="background">Some HCI-ers like Raskin and others have asked about the intuitiveness of the desktop.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="16" relname="span">How intuitive is it?</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="8" relname="antithesis">Many workers do not work in an office environment,</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="15" relname="span">yet the classic desktop interface was designed to support that metaphor.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="15" relname="elaboration">Many people are not familiar with folders, files, and directories.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="13" relname="span">The metaphor may not extend as well as we think.</segment>
        <group id="11" type="span"/>
        <group id="12" type="span" parent="11" relname="span"/>
        <group id="13" type="span" parent="19" relname="span"/>
        <group id="14" type="span" parent="10" relname="evidence"/>
        <group id="15" type="span" parent="14" relname="span"/>
        <group id="16" type="span" parent="13" relname="solutionhood"/>
        <group id="17" type="span" parent="1" relname="evidence"/>
